<interrogationes unitas="friatio">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I hash a string key for a hash table?
    <responsum symbolum="friatio_fnv1a friatio_fnv1a_literis friatio_djb2 tabula_friare_fnv1a">friatio_fnv1a
      takes a chorda; friatio_fnv1a_literis takes a pointer and an
      explicit length (it does not look for a NUL, so pass strlen
      yourself). friatio_djb2 is the other choice. tabula_dispersa
      already wraps FNV-1a as tabula_friare_fnv1a, so a table needs no
      hashing of its own. Both are unseeded and the header marks them
      non-cryptographic, so whoever chooses the keys can force
      collisions.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I compute a SHA-256 over data that arrives in pieces?
    <responsum symbolum="sha256_initiare sha256_addere sha256_finire sha256_friare">Call
      sha256_initiare on a SHA256Contextus, sha256_addere once per
      piece (any sizes), then sha256_finire to get the 32 raw bytes
      (SHA256_DIGEST_MENSURA). sha256_friare does all three for one
      buffer. finire does not reset the context: call initiare again
      before reusing it. SHA-1 (sha1_*) has the same shape.
      Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): sha256_addere and
      sha1_addere write past the context buffer (heap overflow, SEGV)
      when a piece of 2^32 - j bytes or more arrives while j = 1..63
      bytes are already buffered, and an empty piece with a NIHIL
      pointer (e.g. sha256_friare_chorda or sha1_friare_chorda on the
      empty chorda {0, NIHIL}) is undefined behavior that UBSan flags,
      though the digest is correct.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I get a hex string or a constant-time comparison from friatio's SHA-256?
    <responsum symbolum="sha256_finire sigillum_computare sigillum_hex sigillum_aequale sigillum_hmac">No:
      friatio only fills a raw i8 digest array, and it has no HMAC.
      sigillum.h is a second SHA-256 that does: sigillum_computare
      returns a Sigillum, sigillum_hex writes 64 lowercase hex
      characters plus a NUL, sigillum_aequale compares in constant
      time, sigillum_hmac does HMAC-SHA256, and its lengths are
      memoriae_index (sha256_addere takes an i32 per call). Prefer
      sigillum where the hash is an identity or guards a
      secret.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why are there two SHA-256 implementations, friatio and sigillum?
    <responsum symbolum="sha256_friare sigillum_computare" via="include/sigillum.h">They
      serve different jobs, as the sigillum.h header explains:
      friatio hashes to spread keys over a table, while sigillum
      SEALS, a digest that is a content identity (tabularium entities,
      CAS) where collision resistance carries the weight. Hence
      sigillum's Sigillum type, hex output, constant-time
      sigillum_aequale and HMAC. friatio.h does not point to sigillum,
      so a new caller has to know it exists.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I use sha1_friare for anything security related?
    <responsum symbolum="sha1_friare sha1_finire uuidv5_creare">No.
      The header marks SHA-1 obsolete for security; it is here for
      legacy checksums and for UUIDv5, which the standard defines
      with SHA-1 (lib/uuid.c is its consumer). Use SHA-256 for
      anything new.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I compute a CRC32 incrementally over a stream?
    <responsum symbolum="crc32_initiare crc32_addere crc32_finire crc32_calculare">crc
      = crc32_initiare(); then crc = crc32_addere(crc, data, n) for
      each piece; finally crc32_finire(crc) inverts the bits. Feed
      addere the running value, never the finished one.
      crc32_calculare does it in one call. The polynomial is the IEEE
      one (0xEDB88320, reflected), as in ZIP, PNG and
      Ethernet.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what type do the friatio functions return, signed or unsigned?
    <responsum symbolum="friatio_fnv1a crc32_calculare sha256_friare">Unsigned:
      i32 is unsigned in this house (s32 is the signed one), so the
      32-bit results wrap with defined arithmetic and print with %u
      or 0x%08X. Digests are arrays of i8, which is unsigned char. The
      input bytes are hashed as unsigned too, so results do not depend
      on whether the platform's char is signed.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
